{
  "_readme": [
    "The editorial heart of the salary pipeline: which real occupations sit behind",
    "each of the nine YNS categories, and at which experience levels each one is a",
    "sensible thing to show someone.",
    "",
    "This is the ONE part no script can do for you. Everything downstream is",
    "mechanical. Read it as a careers adviser, not as a developer.",
    "",
    "  soc     A Standard Occupational Classification code. CareerOneStop also",
    "          accepts O*NET codes and plain titles, but SOC is the stable key.",
    "  title   What YNS shows the person. Deliberately allowed to differ from the",
    "          official SOC title, which is often bureaucratic ('Social and Human",
    "          Service Assistants'). The official title is preserved in the output",
    "          as soc_title so the mapping stays auditable.",
    "  levels  Which of early | some | experienced | leader this role should appear",
    "          for. A role can appear at several. Wage bands come from percentiles",
    "          of the SAME occupation, so 'early' is not a different job — it is",
    "          the bottom of this one.",
    "  manager Set true when the occupation IS the step up to management. At the",
    "          'leader' level those get the interquartile range (p25-p75) instead",
    "          of p75-p90, because taking the top band of an already-senior job",
    "          counts the seniority twice and badly overstates the number. Ignored",
    "          at every other level.",
    "  band    Optional [lowPercentile, highPercentile] override for one occupation,",
    "          e.g. [\"Pct10\",\"Median\"]. Use sparingly and say why in review.",
    "",
    "THE CODES BELOW ARE A STARTING POINT I DRAFTED, NOT A VETTED CROSSWALK.",
    "Review every line. The build script validates that each code resolves to real",
    "wage data and will list anything that does not, but it cannot tell you that a",
    "code is real and still the wrong choice for the category."
  ],

